          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you. (Here Cordelia addresses her father formally as “Your Majesty.”)\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The expression directly avows Cordelia’s loving state toward Lear. In context, she presents that love as real but limited by the proper filial bond rather than as all-consuming love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal invokes and enacts Cordelia’s existing filial obligation to Lear, rather than making a new promise. She identifies that bond through the duties of a daughter—returning love, obedience, and honour. Lear does not accept this measured formulation as adequate; he tells her to amend it and warns that it may damage her fortunes.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mend your speech a little,\\nLest you may mar your fortunes.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled, carefully qualified answer to Lear’s demand, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Cordelia explicitly frames her response as something she has difficulty putting into speech.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth:\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.78,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowal and invocation of filial duty, the expression has a weak but discernible corrective function: Cordelia resists Lear’s competitive love test by presenting her love as properly bounded rather than rhetorically limitless. Most of its core force is nevertheless covered by T and P.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sure I shall never marry like my sisters,\\nTo love my father all.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to declare which loves him most, linking their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. Cordelia answers her father with a restrained avowal, qualifying her love by her filial bond and rejecting the premise that a married daughter should give all love to her father.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing daughter’s bond: she will return the fitting duties of obedience, love, and honour to Lear. She does not clearly undertake a new future commitment in the focal words. Lear’s immediate uptake is dissatisfaction rather than acceptance; he demands a better speech and warns of harm to her fortunes. Later supplied passages support continuing affection through her care, forgiveness, and grief, but they do not change the original expression into a new promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as Shakespeare’s King Lear, specifically the opening love test and later scenes involving Cordelia and Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The focal TARGET modernises Cordelia’s actual wording, “I love your majesty.” The classification therefore relies on the supplied scene to identify the speaker, addressee, qualification, and institutional setting. The evidence strongly supports affection and filial duty, but it does not establish a separately negotiated undertaking with defined future terms.\"}"
        }
